    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if i see it working on my end but not on a client's end, it's just the ISP/cache thing? it's just weird how it's so inconsistent and you can't really see where the \"break\" is happening. it's like the data is just hiding or something.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, if your IP works locally but not remotely, it is almost certainly because your client's network is still serving cached data from their local DNS provider. That cache acts as a semi-solids shield, effectively hiding the old IP address from new users until the timer expires. Since your location might have a fresh connection while others rely on previous responses, the entire internet effectively becomes this invisible maze of conflicting records.",
